        <body>
            <div xml:id="d48">

                <pb facs="0132" n="76" sameAs="frus:frus1876/pg76"/>

                <head>No. 48.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Scruggs to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Bogota</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-07-17"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 17, 1875</date>.
                        (Received September 10.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 114.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose herewith, for the information of
                    the Department, a printed copy of a translation from Spanish to English of an
                    elaborate and somewhat curious report made to the last Colombian Congress on the
                    subject of the proposed interoceanic ship-canal.</p>
                <p>Although this report is evidently fanciful in many respects, it is, nevertheless,
                    interesting as showing the history of the proposition to unite the two oceans by
                    water communication, and besides contains some statements which are conceived to
                    be of interest in the United States.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WILLIAM L. SCRUGGS.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d48-a1.xml" xpointer="d48-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
